{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And you shall make poles of acacia wood.</b> They are the bearers of the Ark, for by them the Ark was held. And it should have said 'וְעָשִׂיתָ בַדִּים' (and you shall make poles) — rather, by way of allusion it alludes to the sixteen men who are called up to the Torah on Sabbath and on Monday and Thursday, corresponding to the numerical value of 'בַדֵּי.' This is what it says: 'וְצִפִּיתָ אוֹתָם זָהָב' (and you shall overlay them with gold) — that is, day three [ז׳ = גימ\"ל = third day of the week] and day five [ה׳] and day two [ב׳]. A parallel to this we explained in עוֹלְלוֹת אֶפְרַיִם, discourse 261, in the passage about the golden table that required sixteen men to carry it, etc. And the poles shall not be removed, for a covenant has been cut; said HaShem (Isaiah 59:21): 'לֹא יָמוּשׁוּ מִפִּיךָ וּמִפִּי זַרְעֲךָ' (My words shall not depart from your mouth nor from the mouth of your seed), and as it is written (Joshua 1:8): 'לֹא יָמוּשׁ סֵפֶר הַתּוֹרָה הַזֶּה מִפִּיךָ' (This book of the Torah shall not depart from your mouth)."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "The abbreviation רס״ט resolved as 261 by standard gimatria (ר=200, ס=60, ט=9). The verse 'וְצִפִּיתָ אוֹתָם זָהָב' is from Exodus 25:13, immediately following our lemma; the word 'אוֹתָם' is read as 'אוֹתָם' = 'their letters,' yielding ז-ה-ב = third, fifth, second days of the week."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:23:03.584563428Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}